      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>Every site has a story. The daily log is how you prove it. When someone disputes an extension of time, memory loses. A record made on the day wins. A <strong>construction daily report app uk</strong> only works if people use it, so it must be easy.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>No single law needs a daily site diary. Many things in it are required.</li><li>Gaps become a risk when a dispute or claim arrives.</li><li>JCT and NEC contracts assume someone keeps records on site each day.</li><li>Paper is legal. The problem is finding it, reading it, and losing it.</li><li>A digital log only works if it adds no fuss for the person on site.</li><li>RIDDOR records are kept for three years. Most solicitors say keep site records six years. CDM 2015 file content lasts the life of the structure.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Why it matters",
      "heading": "The record that wins arguments",
      "html": "<p>A site diary, site log or daily record is an account of what happened on site each day. It is one of the most useful documents on a job. It is also one of the most neglected.</p><p>When someone disputes an extension of time, the question is what the delay did to the programme. A log showing weather, workforce and what work was possible on given dates answers it. A claim built later from memory does not.</p><p>JCT and NEC both assume records are kept on the day. JCT Minor Works needs written notice of delay events. NEC4 needs early warning notices and a programme.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Instructions",
      "heading": "The verbal order, in writing",
      "html": "<p>The diary also holds verbal instructions from the architect, the client or the structural engineer. On some contracts a verbal instruction is binding. But it is useless as proof unless it was written down when it was given.</p><p>It turns a corridor chat into a dated fact. Six months on, the diary remembers who said what.</p>"
    },
