      "heading": "Questions, answered",
      "html": "<h3>Is a daily report a legal requirement?</h3><p>No single law requires a daily site diary. But many things in it are required. Scaffold inspection records under the Work at Height Regulations. RIDDOR records. CDM health and safety file content. JCT and NEC contracts assume site records are kept. Without them, extension of time and loss and expense claims can suffer.</p><h3>What should a daily report contain?</h3><p>Date and weather. Workers on site by trade. Plant on site. Materials delivered. Work done by area or trade. Instructions received, and from whom. Visitors. Delays and their cause. Safety observations or incidents. Who filled it in.</p><h3>How long should daily reports be kept?</h3><p>No single rule sets this for site diaries. RIDDOR records inside them must be kept for three years. Most solicitors say keep all site records for at least six years after the job ends. CDM 2015 health and safety file content is kept for the life of the structure.</p><h3>Can I do a daily report in WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. You get each question in your usual chat: workforce, plant, weather, work done, instructions. Quickler makes a timed record and a web report. No new app. See <a href=\"/pages/construction-site-inspection-report-uk.html\">construction site inspection report</a> and <a href=\"/pages/how-to-write-site-inspection-report-uk.html\">how to write a site inspection report</a>.</p>"
